WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:11.400
Week nine. Kirana runs her faculty's peer tutoring, and the room is nearly empty.

2
00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:20.400
Her team says: nobody comes any more. That is a complaint. It is not yet a problem she can work on.

3
00:00:20.400 --> 00:00:26.100
Problem solving is a loop with four steps, walked in order.

4
00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:29.100
Understand the problem.

5
00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:32.100
Find the root cause.

6
00:00:32.100 --> 00:00:35.100
Make a plan.

7
00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:38.100
Execute, and adjust.

8
00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:45.400
Most people skip a step, and always the same one. Some stop at the complaint.

9
00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:49.900
Some judge every plan and never make one.

10
00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:54.400
Some have ideas all day, and no dates.

11
00:00:54.400 --> 00:01:00.900
And some act at once, without a diagnosis. Fast, busy, and often wrong.

12
00:01:00.900 --> 00:01:07.800
So Kirana starts at step one, and narrows the complaint with a logic tree.

13
00:01:07.800 --> 00:01:10.800
Who stopped coming?

14
00:01:10.800 --> 00:01:13.800
Since when?

15
00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:17.900
At which stage do we lose them?

16
00:01:17.900 --> 00:01:25.200
The attendance sheets answer: first-years, after midterms, who sign up and then do not come.

17
00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:32.900
A good tree has no gaps and no overlaps: every student sits on exactly one branch.

18
00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:41.900
Then she writes the problem as a gap. Current state: nine first-years a week. Desired state: thirty by the end of term.

19
00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:48.400
And the obstacle: since midterms, the sessions clash with the new lab schedule.

20
00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:53.300
Now it is a problem someone can work on.

21
00:01:53.300 --> 00:01:59.800
A complaint becomes a problem when it has a number and an obstacle.

22
00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:03.100
Notice which step you skip.

23
00:02:03.100 --> 00:02:12.100
Now it is your turn. Take one complaint you made this week and write it as a gap: current, desired, obstacle.
